Fillets and chamfers: the end of a blend at a vertex of three faces
(PerformOneCorner) whose cap on the face at the end leaves that face across
one of its edges and comes back across the same edge, the face beyond that
edge tangent to it, is cut by the face beyond between the two crossings
(mitcad#2229, GitHub mitcad/Mitcad#566).

The case: a block rounded all round keeps a flat face at the end of a
straight edge between two planes (the side of a stepped block rounded by
5 on its sides' and steps' edges: the rounding's core region, reaching 5
from the edge along its two faces and 2.89 along the diagonal), ending in
a rounding tangent to it (a torus band). The edge is chamfered with 4.5 or
4.9: the bevel's cap on the end face, a straight line from the bevel's
contact on one face to that on the other, leaves the end face across the
torus band's edge and comes back across it. Expected: one valid solid, the
body less the bevel's wedge (407.8222 of 509.0686 at 4.5), the end face
kept in two corner pieces, the torus band cut by the bevel. Observed: the
whole cap stored on the end face: the end face trimmed by a line that runs
outside it (its area -0.69, the face's wire crossing itself), the torus
band left whole under the bevel, the shape invalid and 0.0036 smaller.

The origin: the cap is one intersection of the blend's surface with the end
face's surface, from contact to contact, stored on that face alone (as an
end face always covers the blend's section where the blend's ends lie on
its arcs).

- After the cap is computed, where neither end is OnSame, rolled, along an
  edge, on a split face, ridden on or on a seam, and both ends lie on arcs:
  the cap's curve on the end face is intersected with the face's other
  edges. Where exactly one edge is crossed, exactly twice, both crossings
  strictly inside the cap, the cap's middle between them outside the face
  and the face beyond that edge tangent to the end face along it
  (ChFi3d::IsTangentFaces), the cap is stored in three pieces: on the end
  face up to the first crossing, on the face beyond between the crossings
  (the blend's surface intersected with that face's, ChFi3d_ComputeCurves,
  between the crossings' points on both), and on the end face from the
  second crossing. The face beyond keeps the side the end face keeps (its
  orientation from the two surfaces' normals at the crossing), the edge
  between them is cut at both crossings, its part between them on the
  corner's side of the cap's chord going.
- With the same conditions, where both of the cap's ends are vertices, the
  two ends of one edge of the end face, and the cap's inside lies outside
  the face, the face beyond that edge tangent to the end face along it
  (the chamfer of 5 there: the bevel's contacts reach the end face's
  corners, its cap is the chord of the band's edge, observed: valid but
  the end face trimmed by the chord outside it, area -3.06, the band
  uncut, 0.058 mm3 short): the cap is stored on the face beyond alone,
  the blend's surface intersected with that face's from vertex to vertex,
  and the end face goes with its edges, cut at their vertices as a
  consumed face's are.
- ChFi3d_ComputeCurves, between given ends (wholeCurv false, as the end
  of a blend cut by several faces is, PerformIntersectionAtEnd): a line
  that is not periodic whose ends are found the other way round (Uf above
  Ul) is trimmed from Ul to Uf, the inversion that follows running it from
  the first end again. The case, found by the trace below: the chamfer of
  5.5 and 7 there, whose contacts leave the bottom and the back onto the
  half balls before the edge's ends, the end cut by a half ball, the torus
  band and the other half ball. GeomInt's line on a ball is a piece of a
  circle within the surfaces' bounds (1.4858 to 1.6417, the short arc
  between the ends); at the spine's first end its ends were found at 1.6417
  and 1.544, and trimmed from Uf to Ul the piece of the periodic circle
  went round its period (1.6417 to 7.8272): the cut the long way round,
  the bevel face and the balls' faces with wires crossing themselves
  (BRepCheck status 18), their areas negative (the volume right only as
  the long arcs cancelled). At the other end the ends came in order.
- PerformIntersectionAtEnd, choosing where the blend crosses an edge
  between two faces round the vertex: where that edge is the previous one
  again (the end leaving a face across an edge and coming back across it,
  the face between them a lens there) and the blend crosses it elsewhere
  too, not the crossing the previous one took. The case, found by the
  trace below: the rounding of 6 there, its end on a half ball, the torus
  band, the flat end face, the band again and the other half ball; both
  visits of the band's edge took the crossing nearest the vertex, the end
  face between them was passed as at a point and went, and the band's cut
  ran across where the end face's lens is: the band's wire crossing
  itself, four edges in one face, the shape's volume 0.
- A blend exactly as wide as both faces beside its edge where each face
  ends in a half circle (the rounding of 10 there, mitcad#2329): the
  analytic blend's contact lines (straight, on planes) touch their faces
  at one point of an edge each, the faces' boundaries turning away from
  them on both sides. Observed: SplitKPart's hatching found no domain
  ("tangency line out of the face"), the walk that followed found no
  start (each of its tries had a contact a micrometre outside its face,
  "StartSol echec"), and the fillet failed. Now:
  - SplitKPart (HatchAtTouch): where a side's straight contact line has
    no domain, the line moved into the face by the hatcher's shift finds
    exactly one, across one edge of the face (its ends on that edge), the
    edge's point nearest the line between those crossings lies within a
    tenth of the shift of it, and the line is outside the face at every
    other sample between the crossings: the domain is that point, of no
    length, its ends on that edge at the touching point. Only where both
    sides touch so (a touch on one side, the other's contact inside its
    face, is refused as before), the two domains meeting within
    Precision::PIntersection(). A line crossing two edges about the
    vertex between them is not taken (refused as before).
  - The piece of no length is ended at both ends as a piece whose
    contacts leave their faces across an edge (PerformOneCorner's end
    cuts, above), each end cut from the touching point.
  - ChFi3d_FilDS, a stripe of that one SurfData whose two ends on a side
    are on the same edge at the same point (not a vertex) and whose
    contact there has no length: no edge on that side's face (its line
    left out, the face going whole); the stripe's two points on that side
    made one (the last one's interferences given the first's point), and
    the edge touched cut there with the face off it on both sides (one
    EXTERNAL interference at the point instead of the two ends' FORWARD
    and REVERSED ones, which kept the edge on both sides).
  The case: a ball of 5 swept over a square (slab, two half cylinders, a
  ball), or over the reported design's end-face region (its prism, a torus
  along its arc, balls at the arc's ends), rounded on the back's bottom
  edge with 10: one valid solid of the exact volume (561.3195 of 561.3205
  by vprops for the first, 297.73099 of 297.73097 for the second), the
  bottom and the back gone, no face under 0.01.
- A trace on the standard error while MITCAD_TRACE_FILLET_WALK is set (as
  the walk's trace of patch 2141): each of ChFi3d_ComputeCurves' lines
  between given ends (whether it is periodic, its range, whether each end
  was found on it, the face's bounds, the piece trimmed) and, in
  PerformIntersectionAtEnd, each face at the end (the cut's ends in its
  parameters, its bounds) and the cut on it (its ends and middle).
- Elsewhere nothing changes. No catch is added.

Upstream: changes the ends of blends whose cap leaves a flat end face
across an edge tangent to the face beyond and returns, or runs along
outside it from one end of that edge to the other (invalid or wrong
shapes before), and builds analytic blends whose contacts touch both
faces at one point each (failures before).

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2229.
